{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The point of a prototype is to learn quickly whether an idea is worth pursuing, and the code is meant to be thrown away, so reading it closely mostly wastes time. Run it in a sandbox with fake data and no real credentials, and don't let it quietly turn into the MVP."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo usually lives longer than planned and shapes what stakeholders expect, so someone should understand how it works and confirm it isn't faking anything it claims to do. Skim the core logic and anything that touches real data, secrets, or external services, and check the demo flows by hand or with a few smoke tests."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users mean real data, so security, privacy, and data-integrity bugs now cost something. Lean on tests for behavior, and review line by line wherever a quiet mistake would be expensive: authentication, authorization, payments, data migrations, input handling, and anything storing personal data."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "A team has to maintain this code for years, so every change needs at least one person who understands it well enough to debug it at 2 a.m. AI can write the code, but each change goes through normal line-by-line code review with tests; low-risk work such as test scaffolding or internal tooling can drop to level 3."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Regulated and safety-critical systems need code that a named person can trace, justify, and certify, and accountability can't be handed to a tool. Use AI for suggestions, analysis, test generation, and review assistance, but have engineers write or fully rewrite the shipped code under your existing verification and compliance processes; isolated non-critical support code may go up to level 2 if your regulator allows it."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Read AI-written code more closely as the cost of a hidden defect rises and the code is expected to last longer. Accept it freely in throwaway prototypes, check it with tests plus targeted review in demos and MVPs, review every line in maintained production code, and keep people writing the code in critical systems. Within any stage, set the level by the risk of the specific code rather than the project as a whole: auth, payments, data handling, and security-sensitive paths always get line-by-line review once real users or data are involved. Whatever the level, the person who merges AI-written code owns it as if they wrote it."
}

